  "narrative": {
    "intro": "General Dynamics Information Technology obligated $4B in federal contracts in FY2026 to date. General Dynamics Information Technology won 954 awards so far this fiscal year, with an average award size of $4.2M, and obligations fell 12.8% year over year. Yearly obligations show a rise from $2.4B in FY21 to a peak of $4.8B in FY24, followed by $4.6B in FY25 and the current $4B in FY26. Department of Defense is the largest buyer in FY2026 to date at $1.2B, followed by Department of Health and Human Services at $892M and General Services Administration at $802M. Computer Systems Design Services is the largest market for General Dynamics Information Technology at $1.8B, with Custom Computer Programming Services second at $702M and Engineering Services third at $494M. The firm’s FY2026 to date portfolio concentrates on IT services, custom software development, and engineering support, with sizable work for civilian health agencies and major defense customers. The current totals are fiscal year to date and represent contract obligations, not full contract values when those differ.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has General Dynamics Information Technology's federal business changed year over year?",
        "body": "General Dynamics Information Technology obligated $4B in FY2026 to date, down 12.8% year over year. Obligations grew from $2.4B in FY21 to $4.8B in FY24, then moved to $4.6B in FY25 before the current $4B in FY26. The count of awards in FY2026 to date is 954, with an average award size of $4.2M. These figures cover obligations to date and do not represent full contract values in tables that list award-value figures."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are General Dynamics Information Technology's largest agency customers this fiscal year?",
        "body": "Department of Defense is the largest customer at $1.2B in FY2026 to date. Department of Health and Human Services follows at $892M and General Services Administration at $802M. Several civilian agencies including Department of State ($283M) and Department of the Interior ($191M) also appear in the top ten buyers."
      }
    ]
  },
